# Another World Virtual Machine Implementation Analysis

## CALL Opcode (Lines 596-602)
```c
case 0x04: /* CALL subroutine instruction */
{
    uint16_t addr = fetch_word();
    m_stack->push(PC);
    PC = addr;
    return;
}
```
The implementation pushes the current program counter onto the stack before jumping to the subroutine address.

## pauseThread/BREAK Implementation (Lines 604-611)
```c
case 0x06: /* pauseThread instruction (a.k.a. "break") */
{
    if (current->requested_PC == NO_REQUEST)
        current->requested_PC = PC;

    nextThread();
    return;
}
```
This instruction saves the current PC in the thread's requested state, then yields to the scheduler.

## JMP Implementation (Lines 613-617)
```c
case 0x07: /* jmp to address */
{
    PC = fetch_word();
    return;
}
```
A straightforward unconditional jump that loads a new address into the program counter.

## nextThread Scheduler (Lines 467-492)
```c
void another_world_cpu_device::nextThread()
{
    input_updatePlayer();
    Thread* current;
    do {
        if (++m_currentThread == NUM_THREADS) {
            m_currentThread = 0;
            checkThreadRequests();
            ((another_world_vm_state*) owner())->updateDisplay(0xFE);
        }
        current = &m_threads[m_currentThread];
    }
    while(current->state == FROZEN || 
          current->PC == INACTIVE_THREAD);

    PC = current->PC;
}
```
The scheduler cycles through threads, skipping frozen or inactive ones, and wraps around each frame to process pending requests.

## checkThreadRequests Implementation (Lines 430-453)
```c
void another_world_cpu_device::checkThreadRequests()
{
    if (m_requestedNextPart != 0) {
        initForPart(m_requestedNextPart);
        m_requestedNextPart = 0;
    }

    for (int i=0; i<NUM_THREADS; i++) {
        Thread* thread = &m_threads[i];
        thread->state = thread->requested_state;

        if (thread->requested_PC != NO_REQUEST) {
            if (thread->requested_PC == DELETE_THIS_THREAD)
                thread->PC = INACTIVE_THREAD;
            else
                thread->PC = thread->requested_PC;

            thread->requested_PC = NO_REQUEST;
        }
    }
}
```
This commits pending thread state changes at frame boundaries.

## execute_instruction Dispatch (Lines 516-524)
```c
void another_world_cpu_device::execute_instruction()
{
    debugger_instruction_hook(PC);
    Thread* current = &m_threads[m_currentThread];
    // ... logging and opcode fetching ...
    unsigned char opcode = fetch_byte();
    // ... conditional execution of opcode cases ...
}
```
The dispatcher fetches opcodes and delegates to case handlers for the 26+ implemented operations.

# Another World Virtual Machine Opcodes

## Conditional Jump (0x0A)

The implementation supports 6 comparison modes with variable-length operands:

```cpp
case 0x0A: /* condJmp */
{
    uint8_t subopcode = fetch_byte();
    uint8_t v = fetch_byte();
    int16_t b = read_vm_variable(v);
    uint8_t c = fetch_byte();
    int16_t a;

    if (subopcode & 0x80)
        a = read_vm_variable(c);
    else if (subopcode & 0x40)
        a = c << 8 | fetch_byte();
    else
        a = c;

    bool expr = false;
    switch (subopcode & 7)
    {
        case 0: expr = (b == a); break;  // jz
        case 1: expr = (b != a); break;  // jnz
        case 2: expr = (b > a);  break;  // jg
        case 3: expr = (b >= a); break;  // jge
        case 4: expr = (b < a);  break;  // jl
        case 5: expr = (b <= a); break;  // jle
    }
    uint16_t offset = fetch_word();
    if (expr) PC = offset;
    return;
}
```

## Essential Opcodes

**MOV (0x01):** Copies variable value to destination.

**ADD (0x02):** Sums two variables, stores in destination.

**RET (0x05):** Pops stack and returns to caller.

**setVect (0x08):** Assigns execution target to specified thread.

**DJNZ (0x09):** Decrements variable, jumps if nonzero—essential for loops.

All operands use little-endian 16-bit words with variable-length encoding based on flag bits.

